<div class="modal fade" id="level55Modal" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="level55ModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-dialog-scrollable" style="max-width:800px;">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
        <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
            <img src="https://pg.concordreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/hospital_pin-blue.png" style="width:30px; height:30px;">
            <h5 class="modal-title" id="level55ModalLabel">Hospital</h5>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="Close"></button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Overview</b></h6>
            <p class="text-justify">RMI has two public hospitals, both run by MOHHS. The first is Majuro Hospital in the capital, officially the Leroij Atama Zedkeia Medical Center. The second is Ebeye Hospital on Ebeye in Kwajalein Atoll, officially the Leroij Kitlang Memorial Health Center. MOHHS states that both hospitals &#8220;provide primary and secondary care, but limited tertiary care.&#8221; Each provides inpatient and outpatient care, emergency care, public health clinics, pharmacy, laboratory, radiology and rehabilitation. Majuro Hospital is the main national referral point, and outer-island health centers communicate with it by radio.</p>
            <p class="text-justify"><strong>Note:</strong> The two hospitals are not formally ranked against each other by law. Majuro serves about half the national population and receives most outer-island referrals. Ebeye serves Kwajalein Atoll and is administered through MOHHS&#8217;s Kwajalein/Ebeye Atoll Health Care office, under its own Acting Deputy Secretary. Spellings of the official names vary between sources (for example, &#8220;Leroij&#8221; and &#8220;Lerooj&#8221;), and both hospitals are commonly called Majuro Hospital and Ebeye Hospital. Record both as &#8220;Hospital &#8212; public (MOHHS), primary and secondary care,&#8221; and do not record either as a tertiary facility.</p>
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Role</b></h6>
            <ul>
                <li>Serve as the only inpatient hospitals in the country and the referral points for all outer-island health centers</li>
                <li>Provide emergency, inpatient, outpatient and public-health clinic services, plus ancillary services</li>
                <li>Initiate off-island medical referrals, which must be recommended by the Medical Referral Committee and paid for under the Health Fund (Health Fund Act, Sections 208 and 223); there are roughly 90&#8211;120 off-island referrals a year, mostly for cancer, congenital conditions and orthopedic surgery.</li>
            </ul>
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Clinical Service</b></h6>
            <p><strong>Diagnostic &amp; Support Infrastructure</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Laboratory, radiology, pharmacy, rehabilitation and medical records at both hospitals; Majuro Hospital hosts the national electronic health information system used for surveillance</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Not published in the official sources reviewed</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-justify"><em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: Marshall Islands Health Fund Act of 2002 (7 MIRC Ch. 2), Sections 202, 208 and 223. Hospital names, scope and services come from the MOHHS website (rmihealth.org) and the World Bank&#8217;s primary health care assessment; referral volumes come from a secondary source (infomarshallislands.com).</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="modal fade" id="level33Modal" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="level33ModalLabel" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="modal-dialog modal-dialog-centered modal-dialog-scrollable" style="max-width:800px;">
    <div class="modal-content">
      <div class="modal-header">
         <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
            <img src="https://id.concordreview.com/wp-content/plugins/w2gm/resources/images/map_icons/icons/_new/hospital_pin-green.png" style="width:30px; height:30px;">
            <h5 class="modal-title" id="level33ModalLabel">Clinic / Health Centre</h5>
        </div>
        <button type="button" class="btn-close" data-bs-dismiss="modal" aria-label="Close"></button>
      </div>
      <div class="modal-body">
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Overview</b></h6>
            <p class="text-justify">Outside Majuro and Ebeye, primary care is delivered by a network of outer-island health centers, called dispensaries and managed by MOHHS&#8217;s Outer Islands Dispensary Services. Each center is permanently staffed by a full-time health assistant. Health assistants are locally trained community members and are often the only medical resource on their island. They are supported by radio and telehealth contact with Majuro Hospital, by nurse practitioners, and by visiting outreach teams. MOHHS works with community health councils to involve communities in running health services. MOHHS reports 59 outer-island health centers; the World Bank&#8217;s primary health care assessment reports 56.</p>
            <p class="text-justify"><strong>Note:</strong> The law does not define a &#8220;health center&#8221; or &#8220;dispensary&#8221; as a facility type, and it sets no staffing or equipment standard. MOHHS&#8217;s Bureau of Primary Health Care was established in 1986 to strengthen community-level preventive programs. The World Bank found that outer-island centers have far fewer resources than Majuro and Ebeye: only about 12% had a reliable electricity source and only about 8% had all necessary essential medicines in 2019. For desk research, search &#8220;health center,&#8221; &#8220;dispensary&#8221; and &#8220;health care center&#8221; as names for this tier, and record the count the source uses.</p>
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Role</b></h6>
            <ul>
                <li>Serve as the first point of contact for basic health care on outer atolls and islands, providing a limited essential package of primary care and public-health services</li>
                <li>Refer patients to Majuro or Ebeye Hospital, usually after radio consultation</li>
            </ul>
            <h6 class="fw-bold"><b>Clinical Service</b></h6>
            <p><strong>Typical Bed Capacity</strong></p>
            <ul>
                <li>No inpatient beds reported; outpatient health posts staffed by one health assistant</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="text-justify"><em><strong>Note:</strong> Legal basis: no specific statutory definition; services are provided by MOHHS and financed under the Health Fund Act. Counts and conditions come from the MOHHS website and the World Bank assessment (citing HeRAMS 2019).</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
